00:13:39.000 --> 00:14:12.000
There's snobbery in some of this. If you could have been now, the design can only get worse and worse for your reputation. Because once you're represented by a building you don't believe in, what is the future of living? At the end of 35 minutes, the group concluded that Mr. Brown should reject the client's changes and walk away from the project. This was the conclusion that all of the different architect groups ultimately came to. Having this option is part of the luxury of being a very famous architect, of course. I hope in all of this that Mr. Brown was right. I think he was.
这其中有一些势利。如果你现在妥协了，设计只会对你的声誉越来越不利。因为一旦你被一个你不相信的建筑所代表，生活的未来是什么？35分钟结束时，小组得出结论，布朗先生应该拒绝客户的修改并放弃该项目。这是所有不同的建筑师小组最终得出的结论。当然，拥有这种选择权是作为一位非常著名的建筑师的奢侈的一部分。我希望这一切中布朗先生是对的。我想他是对的。

29
00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:35.000
Ha ha ha! Maybe the client was right. After three grueling days of tests, after all the architects went back to their respective practices all over the country, the study still wasn't quite over. The architects were mailed surveys and questionnaires and they were asked to rank each other. Some architects refused to rank themselves and their peers. Others jumped right in.
哈哈！也许客户是对的。经过三天艰苦的测试，在所有建筑师回到全国各地的各自事务所后，研究仍然没有完全结束。建筑师们收到了邮寄的调查问卷，并被要求互相排名。一些建筑师拒绝给自己和同行排名。其他人则直接参与了。

30
00:14:35.000 --> 00:15:03.000
Philip Johnson raided himself number one. As surprisingly, but Sarah and Enraged Johnson number two, after himself, consider number one. But the fact that people put themselves number one, it also talks about the conception of themselves clearly. And the architects' conceptions of themselves matched with the findings from a lot of the other studies that I-PAR had done over the years. The researchers began to notice certain patterns
菲利普·约翰逊把自己排在第一位。令人惊讶的是，萨里宁把约翰逊排在第二位，仅次于他自己，认为自己是第一。但人们把自己排在第一位的事实，也清楚地说明了他们对自己的认知。建筑师们对自己的认知与iPAR多年来所做的许多其他研究的结果相吻合。研究人员开始注意到某些模式

