across creatives of all professions and all genders. We kept finding that we got the same characteristics associated with the people who were rated highest, whether they were chemists or artists or in other fields. It turns out all different kinds of creative people met similar descriptions. It has an interesting and arresting personality. It is non-conforming and has high aspirations for self. Also, creatives preferred complexity and ambiguity over simplicity and order. Creatives had the capacity to make unexpected connections and see patterns in daily life.
跨越了所有职业和所有性别。我们不断发现，无论他们是化学家、艺术家还是其他领域的专业人士，评分最高的人都具有相同的特征。事实证明，各种各样的创造性人才都符合类似的描述。它具有一种有趣且引人注目的个性。它是不墨守成规的，并且对自己有很高的抱负。此外，创造者更喜欢复杂性和模糊性，而不是简单和秩序。创造者有能力在日常生活中建立意想不到的联系并发现模式。

32
00:15:41.000 --> 00:16:15.000
But creative people didn't necessarily have to have high IQs. Intelligence isn't as important to creativity as people had thought it was. And so I guess that's another thing we found. Average intelligence will do. And it's not as if this is directly related to intelligence, but the school grade point average for the architects was a solid B-. So I par found that creative people tend to be non-conforming, interesting, interested, independent, courageous, and self-centered.
但有创造力的人不一定非要有高智商。智力对创造力的重要性并不像人们想象的那样。所以我想这是我们发现的另一件事。平均智力就足够了。这并不是说它与智力直接相关，但建筑师的学校平均绩点是一个扎实的B-。所以iPAR发现，有创造力的人往往是不墨守成规的、有趣的、感兴趣的、独立的、勇敢的和以自我为中心的。

33
00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:41.000
They tend to be. They don't have to be. You have certain traits and they come up over and over again and that's the creative personality, but it doesn't mean that they're going to be exactly the same. I feel like I kind of could have guessed all those, right? I think it's a lot easier to say, oh, we should know that all along after we've found it. This idea of the creative personality was remarkable at the time. I-Pars findings were lauded and spread.
他们往往是这样。他们不一定非要是这样。你拥有某些特质，它们会反复出现，这就是创造性人格，但这并不意味着他们会完全一样。我觉得我好像能猜到所有这些，对吧？我认为在发现之后说“哦，我们早就应该知道”要容易得多。这种创造性人格的概念在当时是了不起的。iPAR的研究结果受到了赞扬并广为传播。

34
00:16:41.000 --> 00:17:19.000
But over the years, people began to think about personality differently. In the 70s, an argument was emerging that maybe personality wasn't something innate within us, and maybe personalities are the results of our environments and social expectations. Ipar and their mission were called into question. The 1970s was a terrible period for personality psychologists. Ooh, I should tell you about that. We had trouble getting grants, we had trouble getting papers accepted, and we had this horrible empty feeling ourselves.
但多年来，人们开始以不同的方式思考人格。在70年代，一种观点开始出现，认为人格可能不是我们内在固有的，人格可能是我们环境和社会期望的结果。iPAR及其使命受到了质疑。70年代对人格心理学家来说是一个可怕的时期。哦，我应该告诉你那件事。我们很难获得资助，很难让论文被接受，我们自己也有这种可怕的空虚感。

35
00:17:19.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Ipar ended in 1992 and became what is now known as Ipser, the Institute of Personality and Social Research, which has expanded to focus a lot more on social and cultural context beyond analyzing individuals. But just because people aren't using iPAR's methods does not mean the study was all for not. iPAR did prove that creativity wasn't this mysterious, untouchable thing. The studies in iPAR are really sort of iconic in our field. This is Ocean Vartanian of the University of Toronto. His area of expertise is creativity and aesthetics research. If you take any kind of course on creativity either at the undergraduate or graduate level,
iPAR于1992年结束，变成了现在所知的IPSR，即人格与社会研究中心，它已经扩展到更多地关注个人分析之外的社会和文化背景。但仅仅因为人们不再使用iPAR的方法，并不意味着这项研究是徒劳的。iPAR确实证明了创造力不是那种神秘的、不可触碰的东西。iPAR的研究在我们领域确实是标志性的。这是多伦多大学的奥申·瓦塔尼安（Oshin Vartanian）。他的专业领域是创造力和美学研究。如果你在本科或研究生阶段修读任何关于创造力的课程，

